NNAAMMEE
     lloonnggrruunn — Transmeta(TM) Crusoe(TM) LongRun(TM) support

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     LongRun support is a collection of power saving modes for the Transmeta
     Crusoe chips, similar in scope to Intel's SpeedStep.  The following
     sysctl(8) MIBs control the different CPU modes:

     NNaammee                    TTyyppee       CChhaannggeeaabbllee    DDeessccrriippttiioonn
     _h_w_._c_r_u_s_o_e_._l_o_n_g_r_u_n       _i_n_t_e_g_e_r    yes           LongRun mode:
                                                      0: minimum frequency
                                                      mode
                                                      1: power-saving mode
                                                      2: performance mode
                                                      3: maximum frequency
                                                      mode
     _h_w_._c_r_u_s_o_e_._f_r_e_q_u_e_n_c_y     _i_n_t_e_g_e_r    no            Current frequency (MHz).
     _h_w_._c_r_u_s_o_e_._v_o_l_t_a_g_e       _i_n_t_e_g_e_r    no            Current voltage (mV).
     _h_w_._c_r_u_s_o_e_._p_e_r_c_e_n_t_a_g_e    _i_n_t_e_g_e_r    no            Processing performance
                                                      (%).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Print the current status:

           % sysctl hw.crusoe

     To set LongRun mode to performance oriented variable frequency mode (less
     power savings):

           # sysctl hw.crusoe.longrun=2

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The Transmeta(TM) Crusoe(TM) LongRun(TM) support first appeared in
     FreeBSD 4.4.

